{
  "concept": "Merge sort builds a sorted array bottom-up by repeatedly merging two already-sorted runs, doubling run length and halving run count at every level of the recursion tree.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Top caption label 'sorted sequence'",
    "Top-rank wide gray box containing 1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7 (the final merged run)",
    "Rank-2 gray box (left) containing 2 4 5 7",
    "Rank-2 gray box (right) containing 1 2 3 6",
    "Rank-3 gray boxes: '2 5', '4 7', '1 3', '2 6'",
    "Bottom rank of eight small single-element squares: 5, 2, 4, 7, 1, 3, 2, 6 left to right",
    "Bottom caption label 'initial sequence'",
    "Thick gray arrows in pairs pointing upward from each pair of child boxes to its parent box (14 arrows total)",
    "Text label 'merge' centered between each arrow pair (7 labels: four at the lowest rank, two at the middle rank, one at the top)",
    "Box width scaling: box width proportional to number of elements it holds",
    "Highlight overlay: active source pair, active arrow pair, and active destination box (used during stepping)",
    "Cursor markers (small triangles) under the front element of each source run during a merge",
    "Output staging strip inside the active destination box showing values appended so far, with empty slots for the rest",
    "Comparison readout annotation, e.g. 'front(left)=2  vs  front(right)=1  →  take right'",
    "Optional color tint on the two duplicate value-2 elements (one from the left half, one from the right half) for the stability trace",
    "Small legend: gray = sorted run, highlighted outline = active merge, tinted = tracked duplicate keys"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "mergeCode",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That merging two sorted runs is a single linear scan driven by one comparison of the two front elements, and that the tie-breaking rule in that comparison is exactly what makes merge sort stable.",
      "label": "Implement merge(left, right) for the highlighted pair",
      "entry_point": "merge(left, right)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "remaining(run)", "does": "how many elements of that run have not been consumed yet" },
        { "op": "front(run)", "does": "peek at the value under that run's cursor — read only, consumes nothing" },
        { "op": "frontLessEqual(a, b)", "does": "true when front(a) <= front(b); the tie case (<=) is what preserves original order" },
        { "op": "take(run)", "does": "pop that run's front element and append it to the output box — the ONLY way to move a value" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "attach your own comment to the current frame of the replay" }
      ],
      "frame": "both source runs with their cursors, the partially filled output box, and the 1-2 elements the op just compared or moved",
      "input": { "label": "Active pair of sorted runs", "default": "left = 2, 4, 5, 7 | right = 1, 2, 3, 6" },
      "sample_code": "function merge(left, right) {\n  // walk both runs, always taking the smaller front\n  while (remaining(left) > 0 && remaining(right) > 0) {\n    if (frontLessEqual(left, right)) {\n      take(left);   // ties go LEFT -> stable\n    } else {\n      take(right);\n    }\n  }\n  // one run is exhausted; drain the other\n  while (remaining(left) > 0) take(left);\n  while (remaining(right) > 0) take(right);\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function merge(left, right) {\n  while (remaining(left) > 0 && remaining(right) > 0) {\n    // BUG: strict '<' sends TIES to the right run,\n    // so the 2 from the right half jumps ahead of the 2 from the left half.\n    if (front(left) < front(right)) {\n      take(left);\n    } else {\n      take(right);\n    }\n  }\n  while (remaining(left) > 0) take(left);\n  while (remaining(right) > 0) take(right);\n}",
      "success_check": "The output box holds every element of both runs, is non-decreasing left to right, and equal keys appear in left-run-before-right-run order (the tinted 2 from the left half precedes the tinted 2 from the right half)."
    },
    {
      "id": "mergeLevel",
      "type": "state",
      "teaches": "That each level of the tree doubles the length of the sorted runs and halves their count, so only log n levels of merging are needed.",
      "label": "Merge-sort level",
      "states": ["initial sequence", "merge to pairs", "merge to runs of 4", "final merge", "sorted"],
      "default": "initial sequence"
    },
    {
      "id": "stabilityTrace",
      "type": "toggle",
      "teaches": "That stability is a property of the comparison inside merge, not of the final sorted values — the two equal 2s are distinguishable and keep their original relative order.",
      "label": "Track duplicate 2s (stability trace)",
      "default": false
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Eight one-element runs",
      "narration": "The bottom rank shows the input 5 2 4 7 1 3 2 6 as eight single-element boxes. A one-element sequence is already sorted, so every one of these is a valid sorted run.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "initial sequence", "stabilityTrace": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "First merge pass: runs of 2",
      "narration": "Adjacent singletons are merged pairwise into 2 5, 4 7, 1 3 and 2 6. Four merges, each a single comparison — the boxes get twice as wide and half as numerous.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "merge to pairs", "stabilityTrace": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Second pass: runs of 4",
      "narration": "Two merges combine the pairs into 2 4 5 7 and 1 2 3 6. Watch the cursors: each merge is one left-to-right sweep, never a rescan.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "merge to runs of 4", "stabilityTrace": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Write merge() for the final pair",
      "narration": "Now implement merge(left, right) yourself on 2 4 5 7 and 1 2 3 6. Each take() appends one value to the top box, and the replay shows exactly which comparison caused it.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "final merge", "stabilityTrace": false }
    },
    {
      "title": "Ties and stability",
      "narration": "Turn on the duplicate trace: the two 2s are tinted differently. With '<=' the left 2 goes first and order is preserved; swap to strict '<' in the buggy version and the duplicates silently swap places.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "final merge", "stabilityTrace": true }
    },
    {
      "title": "Sorted sequence",
      "narration": "The top box reads 1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7. Three levels of merging over eight elements — log n passes, each touching every element once, giving n log n work.",
      "state": { "mergeLevel": "sorted", "stabilityTrace": true }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Merging is cheap because both inputs are already sorted — you only ever compare the two front elements — so the entire cost of merge sort is n comparisons per level times log n levels.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Four ranks stacked bottom-to-top on a white background, all boxes light gray with thin black borders and serif numerals. Bottom rank: eight small equal squares (5, 2, 4, 7, 1, 3, 2, 6) evenly spread across the full width, with the caption 'initial sequence' centered below. Third rank: four boxes of double width ('2 5', '4 7', '1 3', '2 6'), each centered above its two children. Second rank: two boxes of quadruple width ('2 4 5 7' on the left, '1 2 3 6' on the right). Top rank: one very wide box '1 2 2 3 4 5 6 7' centered, caption 'sorted sequence' above it. Every parent receives two thick medium-gray arrows angling up-inward from its two children, with the word 'merge' in serif text centered between each arrow pair. Box width should visibly double from rank to rank while box count halves — that geometric relationship is the point of the figure. For the interactive layer: the active merge highlights its two source boxes, its arrow pair, and the destination box with a saturated accent outline (e.g. blue), dims the rest of the tree, and draws small cursor triangles under the front element of each source run; the destination box fills slot by slot as take() fires. When the stability trace is on, tint the 2 originating in the left half one color (e.g. orange) and the 2 originating in the right half another (e.g. teal) and carry those tints all the way up into the final box."
}

